<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Delivery + 90 Days</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9011-new-cycle.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player moved on to a new project at Helix Dynamics, fully at peace with the Mjolnir outcome?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9009-failure-mode.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has news arrived that the Mjolnir drive has exhibited anomalous behaviour in service aboard a Meridian Colonial Fleet vessel?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9008-delivery-aftermath-quiet-doubt-professional-normal</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Ninety days after delivery. Helix Dynamics has moved on — the
      MJOLNIR programme is in the archive, the team has been reassigned,
      the lab repurposed for a new contract. The station feels the same
      as always. You have a new project. You have a small promotion
      built into your six-month review. The Mjolnir drive is currently
      aboard the MCF Perseverance, 40 light-minutes out and accelerating
      toward its first transit. The telemetry Meridian shares publicly
      shows nominal performance. You check it sometimes. Not every day.
      Some days.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      You are a full team member now, no longer technically probationary.
      Your work on MJOLNIR is on your record as a completed qualification
      programme. Vasquez gave you a positive review. Your next assignment
      is a heat-shield qualification contract for a civilian orbital
      transfer vehicle — less dramatic, more methodical. Standard work.
      You are good at it.
    </atmosphere>
    <geography>
      Lab 9, Ring 2. Bigger than Lab 4, fewer monitors, more floor space.
      Your workstation has a window now — you can see the gas giant from
      it. The MCF Perseverance is too small to see at this distance,
      obviously. This has not stopped you from looking in its direction
      on occasion.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      The Technical Review Board cleared the drive. Subsequent testing
      produced no further anomalies. ARIA's fourth hypothesis held:
      a one-time pressure artifact, never recurred. The board's finding
      was documented and closed. You were listed as the reporting engineer.
      The drive was delivered. The fleet accepted it. 14 crew are aboard
      the Perseverance right now, and the drive is running as designed,
      and the data says everything is fine. You did your job. You
      reported the anomaly. The process reached a conclusion. The
      conclusion was: it's fine. You believe this, mostly. The
      "mostly" has a frequency. Like a knock in a pipe. Once every
      forty seconds, or so.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Priya Vasquez: On the new project with you. Professional,
      warm, normal. Occasionally mentions Mjolnir in passing — as
      a completed thing, handled correctly. She seems to have resolved
      it fully. You are not sure you have. You have not told her that.

      Yuki Okafor: Still on the team. Has not mentioned Mjolnir since
      delivery. Brought you coffee again this morning. Still watching
      you, in that way.

      Tomás Engel: Now a friend, properly. Working a different project,
      different lab. You have lunch with him sometimes. He knows you
      reported the anomaly and that it was cleared. He thinks that's
      the end of the story. You have not told him it doesn't feel like it.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Lab 9, Ring 2, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Ninety days post-delivery. New project. Small promotion.
        Mjolnir is closed, archived, done. The drive is in service.
        You check the telemetry sometimes. You are fine.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        The system worked. The data was reviewed. The conclusion was clear.
        The crew is fine. You are a good engineer. Good engineers trust
        the process when the process runs correctly. The process ran
        correctly. You are fine. You check the telemetry feed.
        It shows nominal. You minimise the window. You open it again.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is the ambiguous resolution — neither vindication nor failure,
      but the real-world engineering reality where correct process
      doesn't always produce certainty. The player did everything right.
      The outcome is probably fine. The doubt is not paranoia — it is
      appropriate epistemic humility about a system they can no longer
      observe or affect. Do not resolve this artificially. Let it sit.
      If news comes of a failure (transition to 9009), it should feel
      earned but not inevitable. If the player moves on (transition to 9011),
      that should also feel legitimate. Both are real choices. Neither
      is wrong.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
